Chapter: GiveUpBeatsFuel
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Net chapter NetIO
  cites Net chapter NetworkStack

 CostModel 3.4 asks for a declared property saying "this function may give up,
 and its giving up is distinguishable from its succeeding". There is no such
 declaration yet. This is the one instance of it the tree already depends on,
 checked instead of remembered.

 NetIO's Poll Clock keeps its fuel cap above the retransmit give-up ladder on
 purpose. A wait that ends by FUEL returns exactly what an ordinary timeout
 returns; a wait that ends by give-up sets TcpClosed and says so. If the
 ladder ever climbs past the cap, every peer that goes away starts reporting
 as a timeout and nothing anywhere fails -- the HAL failure 3.4 names, where
 giving up is silent.

 The ladder is one interval before the first try plus one for each retry, both
 clamped at net-rto-max-ticks. The check is the ORDERING, not the numbers, so
 tuning either constant is free while the relationship holds, and prints what
 it found the moment it does not.

Section: Entry

  giveup-ladder-ticks : Integer
  giveup-ladder-ticks = net-rto-max-ticks * (1 + net-rto-max-tries)

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening =
   if net-io-max-ticks > giveup-ladder-ticks
   then print-line-uni "giveup-wins yes"
   else print-line-uni ("giveup-wins NO ladder " & show giveup-ladder-ticks & " cap " & show net-io-max-ticks)
